{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224226","model_call":14,"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_209430111#6_479226991"},"record":{"query":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_209430111#6_479226991","op":"document"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_209430111#6_479226991","document":"What Is a ‘Gun-Free Zone,’ and What's Behind the Movement to Get Rid of Them? ... What Is a ‘Gun-Free Zone,’ and What’s Behind the Movement to Get Rid of Them? Mass Shooting What Is a ‘Gun-Free Zone,’ and What’s Behind the Movement to Get Rid of Them? What is a gun-free zone? Who decides where guns are allowed? What about private businesses, like Starbucks or Target? Can they declare themselves gun-free zones? Why would someone want to be armed while grocery shopping? What evidence is there that gun-free zones attract mass shooters? How often have good guys with guns stopped mass shootings? Is there a public safety risk to more people taking up arms to prevent the next mass shooting? Kerry Shaw email  ... At least nine states have extended the exception to teachers who have concealed-carry permits. In reality, few public spaces are truly gun-free, even gun-free zones. ( For the purposes of this explainer, we will use the blanket term, understanding that there are often exceptions.) Gun-free zones can also include courthouses, jails, airports, and sports arenas. Last year, the military eased restrictions on carrying private guns on bases, allowing some service members to holster their own weapons, despite opposition from the Army’s highest-ranking official. Who decides where guns are allowed? Gun laws are different in every state. In 2008, the U.S. Supreme Court ruled for the first time that the Second Amendment should be interpreted to permit private citizens to own guns and keep them in their homes. The court left it to states to decide who can carry firearms and where. Florida, for example, forbids guns in airport terminals — but dozens of other states, including Missouri and Oregon, permit them.","ok":true,"op":"document"},"time":15},"model_calls":14,"document_judgments":13,"relevance_3":5,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
