{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224226","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"right-to-carry\" \"public carry\" \"concealed carry\") (+ \"violent crime\" homicide murder assaults) (+ increase increases increased) (+ study research))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"right-to-carry\" \"public carry\" \"concealed carry\") (+ \"violent crime\" homicide murder assaults) (+ increase increases increased) (+ study research))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_74129727#8_127652789","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3687","rank":1,"snippet":"such laws saw \"significant\" increases in violent crime, including murders, and property crime. Gun prevalence in a state is associated with higher rates of police using lethal force. From May 2007 to January 2017, there were 31 instances nationwide during which a concealed-carry permit holder killed three or more individuals in a single incident. A 2016 Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health study found that violent crime was more than 12% higher in right-to-carry states and it increased by 1.1% each year. In the three years after states adopted <cover>right-to-carry laws, violent crime increased by 6.5%, the study </cover>found, determining that the laws increase crime even if permit holders aren't the ones committing it. \"I think most Americans feel like what a person does in their home doesn't matter. A home is their castle,\" said David Chipman, a former Alcohol, Tobacco and Firearms agent for 25 years and senior policy adviser at Gifford's Law Center to Prevent Gun Violence. \"\" , \"start_char\": 5776.000000 , \"title\": \"Kentucky's new concealed carry law: What it means for gun-loving state","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_74129727#8_127652789","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":8376},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":16,"relevance_3":5,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
