{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224226","model_call":18,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3687"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3687"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_74129727#7_127650898","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3687","rank":2,"snippet":"\"You no longer need a permit for concealed carry. What that means for gun-loving Kentucky You no longer need a permit for concealed carry. What that means for gun-loving Kentucky Kentucky's new concealed carry law: Here's what you should know Safety concerns abound over new law Violent crime increases, study says 'Little change' expected by advocates from new law What you need to know \" , \"segment\": \"A short temper is not a disqualifier for getting a gun unless you've done something criminal.\\\" <cover>Violent crime increases, study says Recent studies show that states with right-to-carry </cover>concealed laws have seen increases in violent crime since those laws went into effect. A 2018 study by the Bureau of Economic Research, a nonprofit, nonpartisan organization based at Harvard University and governed by appointees from universities across the country, found that violent crime increased 13% to 15% over a 10-year period in those states. The research replicated and expanded a popular — and widely debated — 1997 study that many state legislatures have used to adopt right-to-carry laws. It found that right-to-carry laws have little to no impact on ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_74129727#7_127650898","rank":2,"relevance":3}]},"time":6},"model_calls":18,"document_judgments":16,"relevance_3":5,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
