{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224226","model_call":28,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ rand \"johns hopkins\" \"national bureau\" \"nber\") (+ \"right-to-carry\" \"concealed carry\" \"shall-issue\") (+ \"violent crime\" homicide assault murder) (+ increase increased evidence))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ rand \"johns hopkins\" \"national bureau\" \"nber\") (+ \"right-to-carry\" \"concealed carry\" \"shall-issue\") (+ \"violent crime\" homicide assault murder) (+ increase increased evidence))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_238927582#0_252410518","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3694","rank":1,"snippet":"concealed weapon ( CCW ), is the practice of carrying a weapon (such as a handgun) in public in a concealed manner, either on one's person or in close proximity. CCW is often practiced as a means of self-defense. It is illegal in many states to carry a concealed handgun without first obtaining a permit from a designated government authority at the state and/or local level. Permits may be difficult to obtain in some areas. A comprehensive 2004 literature review by the National Academy of Sciences concluded that there was no <cover>evidence that concealed carry either increases or reduces violent crime. A 2020 review by the Rand </cover>Corporation concluded that there is evidence that concealed carry either has no impact or that it may increase violent crime, whereas there was no qualifying evidence that concealed carry decreased violent crime. Contents 1 History 2 State laws 2.1 Permitting policies 2.1.1 Unrestricted 2.1.2 Shall-issue 2.1.3 May-issue 2.1.4 No-issue 2.1.5 Concealed carry on U.S. military installations 2.1.6 Concealed carry on Native American Reservations 2.1.7 Limitations on concealed carry 2.2 Training requirements 2."},"time":1069},"model_calls":28,"document_judgments":25,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
